the air-sea interface and within the\nL17@P0: 1\nL18@P0: Ocean Physics Laboratory, Department of Geography, University of\nL19@P0: California, Santa Barbara, California, USA. 2\nL20@P0: ISDGM, Venice, Italy.\nL21@P0: 3\nL22@P0: School of Mathematics and Statistics, University of New South Wales,\nL23@P0: Sydney, Australia. 4\nL24@P0: Department of Physics, University of Miami, Coral Gables, Florida,\nL25@P0: USA. 5\nL26@P0: College of Oceanic and Atmospheric Sciences, Oregon State\nL27@P0: University, Corvallis, Oregon, USA. 6\nL28@P0: Now at Scottish Association for Marine Science, Scottish Marine\nL29@P0: Institute, Oban, UK. 7\nL30@P0: Department of Geography, University of California, Santa Barbara,\nL31@P0: California, USA. 8\nL32@P0: Sea Engineering, Santa Cruz, California, USA.\nL33@P0: 9\nL34@P0: Remote Sensing Solutions, Incorporated, Pasadena, California, USA.\nL35@P0: 10Institute of Sound and Vibration Research, University of\nL36@P0: Southampton, Southampton, UK. 11Institute of Oceanology, Polish Academy of Sciences, Sopot, Poland. 12IGPP, UCLA, Los Angeles, California, USA. 13Graduate School of Oceanography, University of Rhode Island,\nL37@P0: Narragansett, Rhode Island, USA. 14NASA Goddard Space Flight Space Center, Science Systems and\nL38@P0: Applications, Incorporated, Greenbelt, Maryland, USA. 15Physics and Astronomy, University of Victoria, Victoria, British\nL39@P0: Columbia, Canada. 16Mer Molécules, Facultés des Sciences et Techniques, Université de\nL40@P0: Nantes, Nantes, France. 17Institute of Ocean Sciences, Fisheries and Oceans Canada, Sidney,\nL41@P0: British Columbia, Canada. 18Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California Institute of Technology,\nL42@P0: Pasadena, California, USA. 19Earth Research Institute, University of California, Santa Barbara,\nL43@P0: California, USA. 20Department of Physics and Astronomy, Texas A&M University,\nL44@P0: College Station, Texas, USA.\nL45@P0: 21Lamont Doherty Earth Observatory, Columbia University, Palisades,\nL46@P0: New York, USA. 22Scripps Institution of Oceanography, University of California, San\nL47@P0: Diego, La Jolla, California, USA. 23Department of Oceanography, Dalhousie University, Halifax, Nova\nL48@P0: Scotia, Canada. 24Department of Mechanical Engineering, Center for Ocean\nL49@P0: Engineering, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge,\nL50@P0: Massachusetts, USA. 25Physics Department, University of Miami, Miami, Florida, USA. 26Biological Sciences Department, California Polytechnic State\nL51@P0: University, San Luis Obispo, California, USA. 27Mediterranean Institute of Oceanography, Université d’Aix-Marseille,\nL52@P0: Marseille, France. 28Oil Spill Recovery Institute, Cordova, Arkansas, USA. 29Centre for Materials and Coastal Research, Helmholtz-Zentrum\nL53@P0: Geesthacht, Geesthacht, Germany. 30Department of Computer Science, University of Massachusetts,\nL54@P0: Amherst, Massachussets, USA. 31Department of Civil Engineering, Johns Hopkins University,\nL55@P0: Baltimore, Maryland, USA. 32CRI-Middleware, Tokyo, Japan. 33In-Situ Marine Optics P/L, Bibra Lake, Western Australia, Australia. 34Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, University of California,\nL56@P0: San Diego, La Jolla, California, USA. 35Marine Physical Laboratory, Scripps Institution of Oceanography,\nL57@P0: University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, California, USA. 36WET Labs, Incorporated, Narragansett, Rhode Island, USA. 37Satlantic LP, Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada. 38Marine Science Institute and Department of Geography, University of\nL58@P0: California, Santa Barbara, California, USA. 39Naval Research Laboratory, Stennis Space Center, Mississippi, USA. 40Department of Ocean, Earth and Atmospheric Sciences, Old\nL59@P0: Dominion University, Norfolk, Virginia, USA. 41Now at WesternGeco, Houston, Texas, USA. 42Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts,\nL60@P0: USA. 43WET Labs, Incorporated, Philomath, Oregon, USA.\nL61@P0: Corresponding author: T. Dickey, Ocean Physics Laboratory,\nL62@P0: Department of Geography, University of California, Santa Barbara,\nL63@P0: CA 93106, USA. (tommy.dickey@opl.ucsb.edu)\nL64@P0: ©2012. American Geophysical Union. All Rights Reserved.\nL65@P0: 0148-0227/12/2012JC007964\nL66@P0: JOURNAL OF GEOPHYSICAL RESEARCH, VOL. 117, C00H20, doi:10.1029/2012JC007964, 2012\nL67@P0-1: C00H20 1 of 39upper ocean. Recent developments in several technologies and modeling capabilities have\nL68@P1: enabled the investigation of a variety of fundamental and applied problems related to\nL69@P1: upper ocean physics, chemistry, and light propagation and utilization in the dynamic\nL70@P1: near-surface ocean. The purpose here is to provide background for and an introduction\nL71@P1: to a collection of papers devoted to new technologies and observational results as well\nL72@P1: as model simulations, which are facilitating new insights into optical variability and\nL73@P1: light propagation in the ocean as they are affected by changing atmospheric and\nL74@P1: oceanic conditions.\nL75@P1: Citation: Dickey, T., et al. (2012), Introduction to special section on Recent Advances in the Study of Optical Variability\nL76@P1: in the Near-Surface and Upper Ocean, J. Geophys. Res., 117, C00H20, doi:10.1029/2012JC007964.\nL77@P1: 1. Introduction\nL78@P1: [2] Many of the papers in this special section were stimu\u0002lated by research conducted during the Radiance in a\nL79@P1: Dynamic Ocean (RaDyO) program [Dickey et al., 2011]\nL80@P1: while others are from related studies. One of the primary\nL81@P1: goals of RaDyO was to study the problem of underwater\nL82@P1: visibility and imaging of objects across the air-sea interface\nL83@P1: and of objects residing within the upper ocean as affected by\nL84@P1: a broad range of environmental conditions [e.g., Duntley,\nL85@P1: 1963; Mullamaa, 1975; Preisendorfer, 1976; Davies\u0002Colley, 1988; Schippnick, 1988; McLean and Freeman,\nL86@P1: 1996; Jaffe et al., 2001; Zaneveld and Pegau, 2003; Dolin\nL87@P1: et al., 2006; Doron et al., 2007; Dolin and Luchinin, 2008;\nL88@P1: Levin et al., 2008]. This specific problem and other related\nL89@P1: problems, as described below, involve meteorological,\nL90@P1: physical, optical, biological, and chemical processes acting\nL91@P1: over time and space scales as short as milliseconds and mil\u0002limeters. For example, atmospheric conditions involving\nL92@P1: clouds, fog, aerosols, and sun angle affect incident solar\nL93@P1: radiation impinging on the ocean’s surface. The initial fate of\nL94@P1: the impinging light is controlled by the air-sea interface’s\nL95@P1: topography, as modulated by surface capillary and gravity\nL96@P1: waves, and internal gravity waves [e.g., Schenck, 1957;\nL97@P1: Snyder and Dera, 1970; Dera and Olszewski, 1978; Stramski\nL98@P1: and Dera, 1988; Dera, 1992; Dera and Stramski, 1993;\nL99@P1: McLean and Freeman, 1996; Stramska and Dickey, 1998;\nL100@P1: Zaneveld et al., 2001a, 2001b; Wijesekera et al., 2005; You\nL101@P1: et al., 2009, 2010], and as affected by the presence of\nL102@P1: surfactants [e.g., Wurl et al., 2009] and bubbles [e.g.,\nL103@P1: Stramski, 1994; Zhang et al., 1998; Stramski and Tegowski,\nL104@P1: 2001; Terrill et al., 2001; Wolk et al., 2002; Terrill and\nL105@P1: Lewis, 2004; Zhang et al., 2011]. Light propagation within\nL106@P1: and exiting the upper ocean is also affected by organisms,\nL107@P1: particulate and dissolved materials including colored dis\u0002solved organic matter, physical, chemical,and biological\nL108@P1: processes, inherent optical properties (IOPs), and apparent\nL109@P1: optical properties (AOPs) along with characteristics of the\nL110@P1: molecular and turbulent boundary layers on both sides of the\nL111@P1: air-sea interface. These layers are dynamic and affected by\nL112@P1: wind-forcing, waves, stratification, chemical and particulate\nL113@P1: composition of seawater, optical properties, bubbles, foam,\nL114@P1: and whitecaps (W. K. Melville et al., The impact of surface\nL115@P1: wave breaking on imaging through the ocean surface, man\u0002uscript in preparation, 2012).\nL116@P1: [3] There are several other relevant problems that relate to\nL117@P1: this research and require similar data sets and modeling\nL118@P1: approaches. These include the fundamental problem of\nL119@P1: radiative transfer (e.g., light propagation across the air-sea\nL120@P1: interface and within the upper layer [e.g., Kattawar et al.,\nL121@P1: 1976; Shifrin, 1988; Kattawar and Adams, 1989; Dera,\nL122@P1: 1992; Kirk, 1994; Mobley, 1994; Walker, 1994; Thomas\nL123@P1: and Stamnes, 1999; Zhai et al., 2008a, 2008b]); marine ani\u0002mal vision [e.g., McFarland and Loew, 1983], phytoplankton\nL124@P1: responses to fluctuating light fields [e.g., Frechette and\nL125@P1: Legendre, 1978; Walsh and Legendre, 1983; Legendre et al.,\nL126@P1: 1986; Queguiner and Legendre, 1986; Stramski et al., 1993;\nL127@P1: Kirk, 1994], air-sea exchanges of momentum, heat, and gases\nL128@P1: [e.g., Thorpe, 1982; Wanninkhof, 1992; Woolf, 1997, 2005;\nL129@P1: Weller et al., 1998; Wanninkhof and McGillis, 1999;\nL130@P1: Wanninkhof et al., 2004; Yu and Weller, 2007; Berry and\nL131@P1: Kent, 2009; Brooks et al., 2009], and the interpretation of\nL132@P1: radiometric data collected in the ocean from various plat\u0002forms including imagers mounted on aircraft and satellites\nL133@P1: [e.g., Fraser et al., 1980; McLean and Freeman, 1996;\nL134@P1: Zaneveld et al., 2001a, 2001b; Zibordi et al., 2004;\nL135@P1: Twardowski et al., 2007a; Zaneveld et al., 2005; Lewis,\nL136@P1: 2008; Gernez and Antoine, 2009].\nL137@P1: [4] Fluctuations in the underwater light field caused by\nL138@P1: surface waves have been a subject of earlier theoretical and\nL139@P1: experimental research. The models of Schenck [1957] and\nL140@P1: Snyder and Dera [1970] identified mechanisms for produc\u0002ing flashing light, which are associated with fluctuations in\nL141@P1: sea surface topography, namely its elevation, surface slope,\nL142@P1: and surface curvature. These physical processes are highly\nL143@P1: complex and can invalidate simplifying assumptions; thus,\nL144@P1: the solution of the theoretical problem is challenging. Light\nL145@P1: fluctuations just beneath the ocean surface are most intense\nL146@P1: under sunny skies accompanied by relatively weak winds\nL147@P1: and low sea states when wave focusing of direct solar rays\nL148@P1: dominates. The intensity and high-frequency content of\nL149@P1: these fluctuations decrease rapidly with depth within the top\nL150@P1: several meters of the ocean [e.g., Dera and Gordon, 1968;\nL151@P1: Snyder and Dera, 1970; Fraser et al., 1980; Dera and\nL152@P1: Stramski, 1986].\nL153@P1: [5] Specially designed radiometers and sampling strate\u0002gies are required to fully resolve the maximum focusing at\nL154@P1: near-surface depths (down to about 5–10 m in clear waters),\nL155@P1: because these effects are often characterized by the presence\nL156@P1: of light pulses of very high amplitude and short duration\nL157@P1: [Dera and Olszewski, 1978; Dera and Stramski, 1986;\nL158@P1: Gernez et al., 2011; Darecki et al., 2011]. Most experi\u0002mental work on underwater light fluctuations to date has\nL159@P1: been best suited to examine weaker fluctuations of light that\nL160@P1: occur at depths below the top few meters of the ocean. At\nL161@P1: these depths, even under sunny conditions, the fluctuations\nL162@P1: in instantaneous irradiance generally do not exceed the mean\nL163@P1: C00H20 DICKEY ET AL.: INTRODUCTION C00H20\nL164@P1-2: 2 of 39irradiance by more than 50%, and are often within 10–20%\nL165@P2: of the mean irradiance level. The book by Walker [1994]\nL166@P2: reviews early relevant studies, including several from the\nL167@P2: former Soviet Union. Examples of more recent work include\nL168@P2: Stramska and Dickey [1998] and Gernez and Antoine\nL169@P2: [2009], who noted that if skies are overcast and surface\nL170@P2: illumination is diffuse, underwater light fluctuations are\nL171@P2: weak throughout the entire water column [see also Dera and\nL172@P2: Olszewski, 1967; Stramski et al., 1992]. These fluctuations\nL173@P2: consist primarily of low-frequency components due to sur\u0002face displacements associated with significant surface waves\nL174@P2: and/or swell.\nL175@P2: [6] Interacting processes necessitate novel methods and\nL176@P2: models for imaging and other applications. A major obstacle\nL177@P2: to this line of research has been the inability of sampling and\nL178@P2: computational methods and hardware to sufficiently resolve\nL179@P2: high temporal and spatial scale variabilities, which can have\nL180@P2: very large dynamic ranges (up to at least 6 to 8 orders of\nL181@P2: magnitude) for such a diverse set of physical and optical\nL182@P2: variables. Several previous studies have used instrumenta\u0002tion designed specifically to measure the fast fluctuations\nL183@P2: and associated strongest wave focusing effects occurring at\nL184@P2: near-surface depths under sunny conditions [Dera and\nL185@P2: Olszewski, 1978; Dera and Stramski, 1986; Stramski,\nL186@P2: 1986; Dera and Stramski, 1993]. However, this work has\nL187@P2: been mostly limited to a single light wavelength, typically\nL188@P2: selected for maximum transparency in water. Focusing has\nL189@P2: been found to be most pronounced under weak winds (2–\nL190@P2: 5 m/s) and thus slightly undulating sea surfaces, clear\nL191@P2: atmospheres with low surface irradiance diffuseness of less\nL192@P2: than 40%, and relatively high sun elevation with solar zenith\nL193@P2: angle generally less than 40\u0001 [Dera and Stramski, 1986;\nL194@P2: Stramski, 1986]. Under such conditions, high-amplitude\nL195@P2: pulses of focused light, exceeding the mean level of irradi\u0002ance by more than 50%, have been shown to occur at fre\u0002quencies higher than 100 per minute at near-surface depths.\nL196@P2: These strong focusing events, referred to as light flashes,\nL197@P2: have short durations on the order of 10 ms. Reviews of\nL198@P2: general features of wave-induced light fluctuations are pro\u0002vided in papers by Stramski et al. [1992], Gernez and\nL199@P2: Antoine [2009], Gernez et al. [2011], and Darecki et al.\nL200@P2: [2011].\nL201@P2: [7] Previous work on light fluctuations has been unable to\nL202@P2: include some of the more important physical variables in\nL203@P2: sufficient detail, especially wave characteristics. RaDyO\nL204@P2: researchers are beginning to report significant advances in\nL205@P2: terms of linking light fluctuations to surface waves, because\nL206@P2: specially designed instrumentation and experiments have\nL207@P2: been utilized recently to obtain relevant data sets for devel\u0002oping and parameterizing more realistic models that couple\nL208@P2: radiative transfer, surface waves, and other physical\nL209@P2: dynamics.\nL210@P2: [8] It should be noted that there has been an increased\nL211@P2: awareness of the multiplicity of interdisciplinary processes\nL212@P2: that must be considered in studying problems such as optical\nL213@P2: variability of the ocean. For example, bubbles, which are\nL214@P2: ubiquitous in the world ocean, play important roles for a host\nL215@P2: of processes. The problem of estimating the number of\nL216@P2: bubbles and their size distributions is daunting. MacIntyre\nL217@P2: [1972] forwarded an estimate that some 1018 to 1020 white\nL218@P2: cap bubbles break (and form) per second over the world\nL219@P2: ocean. More recently, Bird et al. [2010] have suggested that\nL220@P2: the number of bubbles may be even greater based on their\nL221@P2: work on daughter bubble cascades (creations of toroidal\nL222@P2: rings of small bubbles upon rupture). The importance of\nL223@P2: bubbles for optics of the ocean has been recognized by a few\nL224@P2: investigators [e.g., Stramski, 1994; Zhang et al., 1998;\nL225@P2: Stramski and Tegowski, 2001; Terrill et al., 2001; Terrill\nL226@P2: and Lewis, 2004; Zhang et al., 2011]. In fact, it has been\nL227@P2: suggested that a large portion of upwelled radiance from the\nL228@P2: ocean surface may be attributed to bubbles and that ocean\nL229@P2: color shifts (toward green wavelengths) can affect ocean\nL230@P2: remote sensing algorithms for chlorophyll a [e.g., Zhang\nL231@P2: et al., 1998]. Bubble size distributions and organic coat\u0002ings of bubbles play important roles as well [e.g., Zhang\nL232@P2: et al., 1998; Stramski and Tegowski, 2001; Zhang et al.,\nL233@P2: 2011]. The recognition of the potential optical effects of\nL234@P2: bubbles has led to the development of new optical and\nL235@P2: acoustical instrumentation with the expressed purposes of\nL236@P2: quantifying bubble effects on light in the ocean; some of\nL237@P2: these are described in this special section [Czerski et al.,\nL238@P2: 2011; Vagle et al., 2012; Twardowski et al., 2012]. Impor\u0002tantly, overall sampling of ocean optical variability has been\nL239@P2: advanced by the utilization of multiple platforms [e.g.,\nL240@P2: Dickey and Bidigare, 2005; Dickey et al., 2006], which\nL241@P2: facilitate the concurrent collection of a plethora of interdis\u0002ciplinary data sets on unprecedented time and space scales.\nL242@P2: The RaDyO program was also devoted to modeling optical\nL243@P2: variability of the surface boundary layer and upper ocean,\nL244@P2: likewise on unprecedented time and space scales. Some of\nL245@P2: this modeling work is introduced in section 5.\nL246@P2: [9] This paper concludes by briefly introducing contribu\u0002tions to this special section categorically as follows: (1)\nL247@P2: observational papers based primarily on results from the\nL248@P2: RaDyO program, (2) modeling papers done in conjunction\nL249@P2: with RaDyO, and (3) papers relevant to the theme of the\nL250@P2: special section that were submitted by authors independently\nL251@P2: of RaDyO. Finally, interested readers are directed to a recent\nL252@P2: survey article by Dickey et al. [2011], which briefly provides\nL253@P2: historical, introductory, and summary information relevant\nL254@P2: to this special section.\nL255@P2: 2. Overview of the RaDyO Observational\nL256@P2: Program\nL257@P2: 2.1. Sites of RaDyO Observations\nL258@P2: [10] Fulfillment of the objectives of RaDyO required that a\nL259@P2: wide range of environmental conditions be observed for the\nL260@P2: development of models that would be useful for diverse\nL261@P2: optical, meteorological, and physical situations [Dickey\nL262@P2: et al., 2011]. The first RaDyO experiment was conducted\nL263@P2: using the Scripps Institution of Oceanography (SIO) pier 6–\nL264@P2: 28 January 2008. This work was primarily intended to test\nL265@P2: new instrumentation in a setting with good access to shore\u0002based technical support and to develop integration of the\nL266@P2: diverse methodologies. However, some scientific results\nL267@P2: have been produced from this experiment as well. The sec\u0002ond experiment was conducted in the Santa Barbara Channel\nL268@P2: (SBC) during the period of September 3–25, 2008. This\nL269@P2: setting was selected because it was expected to provide a\nL270@P2: relatively benign wind-wave regime and easy access to shore\nL271@P2: since several new or prototype instrumentation systems were\nL272@P2: being utilized. The third and final RaDyO experiment took\nL273@P2: place south of the Big Island of Hawaii from 24 August\nL274@P2: C00H20 DICKEY ET AL.: INTRODUCTION C00H20\nL275@P2-3: 3 of 39through September 15, 2009. This location was selected\nL276@P3: because of its climatologically high, persistent wind speeds\nL277@P3: and relatively large sea states, its optically clear waters, and\nL278@P3: its open ocean character with good access to the deep-water\nL279@P3: port of Honolulu. The specific area of operations, which lies\nL280@P3: in the North Equatorial Current (NEC), is characterized by\nL281@P3: relatively modest mesoscale eddy activity and minor island\nL282@P3: effects as compared with areas directly in the wind wake of\nL283@P3: the Hawaiian archipelago [e.g., see Dickey et al., 2008]. The\nL284@P3: field experiments are described in more detail below and on\nL285@P3: the Website http://www.opl.ucsb.edu/radyo/.\nL286@P3: 2.2. Platforms\nL287@P3: [11] The multifaceted, multiscale nature of RaDyO pro\u0002blems necessitated a multiple platform, multidisciplinary\nL288@P3: sampling approach as has been discussed in detail in earlier\nL289@P3: references. The primary platforms used for the RaDyO Santa\nL290@P3: Barbara Channel experiment included: R/P FLIP, R/V Kilo\nL291@P3: Moana, an Odyssey autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV)\nL292@P3: [Moline et al., 2012] (Figure 1), a REMUS AUV [Moline\nL293@P3: et al., 2005, 2007, 2010, 2012; Robbins et al., 2006], and\nL294@P3: a surfactant skimmer called the Lil KM (Figure 1). Also, a\nL295@P3: small aircraft equipped with lidar instrumentation made\nL296@P3: measurements over the site [Reineman et al., 2009]. Because\nL297@P3: of anticipated greater sea states, the Odyssey AUV and the\nL298@P3: Lil KM were not utilized and the REMUS AUV was\nL299@P3: deployed on only a limited basis for the Hawaiian experi\u0002ment. In addition, satellite-deployed sensors were utilized to\nL300@P3: obtain wind, sea surface temperature, color, and surface\nL301@P3: roughness (SAR for Santa Barbara experiment only) data.\nL302@P3: The platforms used for the field experiments are briefly\nL303@P3: described below.\nL304@P3: 2.2.1. R/P FLIP\nL305@P3: [12] R/P FLIP (Floating Instrument Platform, Figure 2),\nL306@P3: originally launched on 2 June 1962, was designed to be a\nL307@P3: highly stable platform for oceanographic research in the\nL308@P3: areas of acoustics, seismic waves, ocean waves, and marine\nL309@P3: biology [Fisher and Spiess, 1963]. However, it has since\nL310@P3: been used for a variety of meteorological, air-sea interaction,\nL311@P3: and physical oceanographic experiments [e.g., Siegel and\nL312@P3: Dickey, 1986; Smith et al., 1987; Pinkel et al., 1991;\nL313@P3: Plueddemann et al., 1996; Smith, 1998; Friehe et al., 2001;\nL314@P3: Rainville and Pinkel, 2006] and for optical measurements in\nL315@P3: 1982 [Siegel and Dickey, 1987; Dickey, 1991]. R/P FLIP is\nL316@P3: essentially a large manned spar buoy when oriented verti\u0002cally (note that it is towed horizontally to experiment sites).\nL317@P3: R/P FLIP measures approximately 108 m in length with\nL318@P3: \u000390 m of its hull extending below the water line when on\nL319@P3: station. The diameter of R/P FLIP’s hull is 6.5 m from the 49\nL320@P3: to 91 m depth; the hull tapers to 4 m at the 20 m depth (see\nL321@P3: schematic in Figure 3 of Fisher and Spiess [1963]). This\nL322@P3: design allows R/P FLIP, in free drift mode, to be minimally\nL323@P3: responsive to wave motion (<10% of surface motion, reso\u0002nant period is \u000325 s and thus displaced away from the\nL324@P3: spectral window of greatest wave motion (about 5–18 s)) as\nL325@P3: documented by Fisher and Spiess [1963], Rudnick [1967],\nL326@P3: Rudnick and Hasse [1971], and Smith and Rieder [1997].\nL327@P3: For example, in ten meter waves R/P FLIP’s total vertical\nL328@P3: motion has been recorded to be less than 1 m (see http://\nL329@P3: www.mpl.ucsd.edu/resources/flip.intro.html for a bibliogra\u0002phy and details concerning R/P FLIP).\nL330@P3: [13] For the Santa Barbara Channel RaDyO experiment,\nL331@P3: R/P FLIP (Figure 2a) was moored in place at 34.2053\u0001 N,\nL332@P3: 119.6288\u0001 W in water of 168 m depth using a 3-point\nL333@P3: mooring [e.g., see Bronson, 1971]. Some tilting of R/P FLIP\nL334@P3: was apparent due to currents and winds. These conditions\nL335@P3: were apparently accompanied by a measurable oscillation in\nL336@P3: R/P FLIP’s heading and depth with an approximately 2 min\nL337@P3: period. Instruments were deployed from R/P FLIP’s booms\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroduction to special section on Recent Advances in the Study of Optical Variability in the Near‐Surface and Upper Ocean - Dickey - 2012 - Journal of Geophysical Research: Oceans - Wiley Online Library (https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2012JC007964)\nciteturn18view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1029/2012JC007964\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2060\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn18view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/phoc/43/10/jpo-d-13-056.1.xml\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n"
